[38;2;124;124;124mLet me think about this in a Dedekind domain. In a Dedekind domain, every [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mnonzero ideal factors uniquely as a product of prime ideals.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, let me set up notation. In a Dedekind domain, ideals factor as:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $I = \prod_{\mathfrak{p}} \mathfrak{p}^{v_\mathfrak{p}(I)}$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m- $(I, a) = \prod_{\mathfrak{p}} \mathfrak{p}^{v_\mathfrak{p}(I,a)}$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNote that $(I, a) = I + (a)$. In a Dedekind domain, $(I, a) = \gcd(I, (a))$ in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe ideal-theoretic sense. Specifically, $v_\mathfrak{p}(I + (a)) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\min(v_\mathfrak{p}(I), v_\mathfrak{p}((a)))$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSimilarly for $(I, b) = I + (b)$, $v_\mathfrak{p}(I + (b)) = \min(v_\mathfrak{p}[0m[38;2;124;124;124m(I), v_\mathfrak{p}((b)))$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, $\mathcal{P}$ is the set of prime ideals appearing in the factorization of [0m[38;2;124;124;124m$(I,a)$ or $(I,b)$. So $\mathfrak{p} \in \mathcal{P}$ iff $v_\mathfrak{p}(I+(a))[0m[38;2;124;124;124m> 0$ or $v_\mathfrak{p}(I+(b)) > 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m$v_\mathfrak{p}(I+(a)) > 0$ iff $\min(v_\mathfrak{p}(I), v_\mathfrak{p}((a))) > [0m[38;2;124;124;124m0$ iff $v_\mathfrak{p}(I) > 0$ and $v_\mathfrak{p}((a)) > 0$.[0m

