[38;2;124;124;124m$v_\mathfrak{p}((b)) > 0$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo $\mathfrak{p} \in \mathcal{P}$ iff ($v_\mathfrak{p}(I) > 0$ and [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$v_\mathfrak{p}((a)) > 0$) or ($v_\mathfrak{p}(I) > 0$ and $v_\mathfrak{p}((b)) [0m[38;2;124;124;124m> 0$).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis means $\mathfrak{p} \in \mathcal{P}$ implies $v_\mathfrak{p}(I) > 0$.[0m

