[38;2;124;124;124mBut wait — I should be careful. The problem says $I$ is a "non-prime ideal." It [0m[38;2;124;124;124mdoesn't say $I$ is nonzero. In a Dedekind domain, the zero ideal is prime (since[0m[38;2;124;124;124ma Dedekind domain is an integral domain). So if $I$ is non-prime, $I \neq 0$, so[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$I$ is a nonzero non-prime ideal, and it has a Dedekind factorization.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAlso, $(a)$ and $(b)$: since $a \notin I$ and $I$ is nonzero, $a$ could be zero?[0m[38;2;124;124;124mIf $a = 0$, then $a \in I$ for any ideal $I$ (since $0 \in I$). But $a \notin [0m
